## Ownership comes first

An organization owns workspaces. A workspace groups resources and controls who can use them. A project can group resources for one application. These boundaries should be explicit in requests and visible in the interface.

<Diagram label="Resource ownership" source={'flowchart TD\n O[Organization] --> W[Workspace]\n W --> P[Project]\n P --> S[Sandbox]\n P --> D[Deployment]\n P --> B[Database]\n W --> C[Computer]'} />

## Separate resources by lifetime

| Resource | Main job | Lifetime |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sandbox | Build, test and execute code | Often disposable |
| Computer | Operate desktop applications | Task or session based |
| Deployment | Serve a published application | Managed release |
| Database or volume | Keep application state | Persistent |

## Connect with bindings

A binding records how an application uses another resource. It might supply a database URL, a storage location or a scoped capability. Keeping these connections explicit makes deployment and recovery easier to inspect.

## Keep identities stable

A name is useful to a person, but a returned identifier names the actual resource. Keep the identifier in operation records. Reusing a familiar display name does not mean you are operating on the same instance.

